**Polymorphism** means "many forms": the same interface or method name behaves differently depending on the context or the object's actual type. It lets you write code against a general type and have the right behaviour chosen automatically.

**Compile-time (static) polymorphism:** method or operator overloading, and templates/generics. The compiler picks the version from the arguments, for example `add(int, int)` versus `add(double, double)`.

**Run-time (dynamic) polymorphism:** method overriding through inheritance with a base-class reference. The method called depends on the real object at run time (virtual functions in C++, normal instance methods in Java).

    abstract class Shape { abstract double area(); }
    class Circle extends Shape {
        double r;
        Circle(double r) { this.r = r; }
        double area() { return Math.PI * r * r; }
    }
    class Rect extends Shape {
        double w, h;
        Rect(double w, double h) { this.w = w; this.h = h; }
        double area() { return w * h; }
    }
    // Shape s = new Circle(2); s.area() calls Circle's version.

The loop `for (Shape s : shapes) total += s.area();` works for any current or future shape without changes, which is the real value (open/closed principle).

